                {
                  "phase": "grammar",
                  "type": "grammar_rule",
                  "content": {
                    "tag": "first_conditional_time",
                    "title": "First conditional and time clauses",
                    "explanation": "El primer condicional habla de situaciones reales o probables en el futuro: If + presente, will + verbo. If it rains, you'll get wet.\n\nLas dos partes pueden cambiar de orden (sin coma si if va en medio): You'll get wet if it rains.\n\nDetrás de when, as soon as, before, after y until también usamos presente, aunque hablemos del futuro: As soon as we get back, I'll put the kettle on (nunca «as soon as we will get back»).\n\nUnless = if not (a menos que): Unless she gets tired of me…",
                    "table": {
                      "headers": [
                        "Estructura",
                        "Ejemplo"
                      ],
                      "rows": [
                        [
                          "If + presente, will + verbo",
                          "If it rains, you'll get wet."
                        ],
                        [
                          "will + verbo + if + presente",
                          "You'll get wet if it rains."
                        ],
                        [
                          "Negativa",
                          "If you don't eat, I'll be offended."
                        ],
                        [
                          "when / as soon as + presente",
                          "As soon as we get back, I'll put the kettle on."
                        ],
                        [
                          "unless (= if not)",
                          "I'll go unless it rains."
                        ]
                      ]
                    },
                    "examples": [
                      "If you don't eat, I'll be offended!",
                      "If I eat any more, I won't be able to move!",
                      "When you come back, I'll make my famous apple tart.",
                      "Unless she gets tired of me first."
                    ]
                  }
                },
                {
                  "phase": "grammar",
                  "type": "grammar_rule",
                  "content": {
                    "tag": "question_tags",
                    "title": "Question tags with all tenses",
                    "explanation": "Las question tags usan el auxiliar del verbo de la frase, en cualquier tiempo:\n• will → won't: You'll come again, won't you?\n• present perfect → have / has: Daniel hasn't phoned yet, has he?\n• past simple → did: She made an apple tart, didn't she?\n• can / should / would: You can drive, can't you? We should go, shouldn't we?\n\nCasos especiales:\n• I am → aren't I?: I'm right, aren't I?\n• Let's → shall we?: Let's go to the cliffs, shall we?\n• Imperativo → will you? / won't you?: Have some more potatoes, won't you?\n• Con never, nobody, nothing (negativos) la tag va en afirmativa: Nobody called, did they?",
                    "table": {
                      "headers": [
                        "Frase",
                        "Tag"
                      ],
                      "rows": [
                        [
                          "You'll come in the summer,",
                          "won't you?"
                        ],
                        [
                          "Daniel hasn't phoned,",
                          "has he?"
                        ],
                        [
                          "Maureen made an apple tart,",
                          "didn't she?"
                        ],
                        [
                          "I'm late,",
                          "aren't I?"
                        ],
                        [
                          "Let's go to the cliffs,",
                          "shall we?"
                        ],
                        [
                          "Nobody likes the rain,",
                          "do they?"
                        ]
                      ]
                    },
                    "examples": [
                      "You'll come again in the summer, won't you?",
                      "We haven't seen the Cliffs of Moher yet, have we?",
                      "Let's have some apple tart, shall we?",
                      "It never stops raining here, does it?"
                    ]
                  }
